<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.17.4: Transforming the Parent Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02052</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.17.4: Transforming the Parent Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>92d7b90b-c32a-41ca-95fb-d5b90ee8693b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_19_09_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00002">Do you see two "eyes" and a smiling "mouth" on the graph? The three arcs on the graph all represent quadratic functions that were initially defined by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, but whose equations were later modified.</para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Write an equation to represent the smile in the smiley face.</para>
            <para id="para-00004">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00006">The mouth can be given by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because it is the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>  shifted 10 units upward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">Write an equation to represent the left eye in the smiley face.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00009">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00010">The left eye can be given by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the vertex is located at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and it opens downward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00011">Write an equation to represent the right eye in the smiley face.</para>
            <para id="para-00012">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00013">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00014">The right eye can be given by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the vertex is located at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and it opens downward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00015">What is the domain for the mouth? Write the domain as an inequality.</para>
            <para id="para-00016">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00017">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00018">For the mouth, the domain is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00019">What is the domain for the left eye? Write the domain as an inequality.</para>
            <para id="para-00020">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00021">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00022">For the left eye, the domain is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> .</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00023">What is the domain for the right eye? Write the domain as an inequality.</para>
            <para id="para-00024">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00025">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00026">For the right eye, the domain is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> .</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
  </content></document>
